    "description": "Persian is an Iranian language with a documented history conventionally divided into Old, Middle, and New Persian. Its writing systems and grammatical structures help explain both its historical continuity and its distinctive character today.",
    "tags": ["Persian", "Iranian languages", "Writing systems", "Grammar", "Language history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:29:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:29:40Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Persian: Language, Script and Grammar | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Persian through its historical stages, changing scripts, sentence structure and the ezafe construction that connects names and descriptions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "language-and-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A language with several historical stages",
        "content": "Persian belongs to the Iranian language group. Here, “Iranian” is a linguistic classification: it includes languages other than Persian and does not simply mean every language spoken within Iran. Scholarly accounts conventionally distinguish Old Persian, Middle Persian and New Persian as successive stages of the same language. Modern Persian belongs to the last of these stages; Farsi, Dari and Tajiki appear within the broader Persian linguistic tradition. [s1]\n\nThese historical labels are useful signposts, rather than evidence that people abruptly changed languages at a political turning point. They also should not be extended indiscriminately to neighboring languages. Kurdish, for example, is another Iranian language, rather than a modern form of Persian. Understanding that distinction makes Iran's linguistic landscape easier to read: a shared language family can contain separate languages with their own histories. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scripts-and-reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The script is part of the history",
        "content": "Persian has not always been written in the alphabet familiar from modern Iranian books. Old Persian inscriptions used cuneiform. Middle Persian used writing systems derived from Aramaic, while New Persian came to be written in a modified Arabic script. The Library of Congress also documents Persian written in Hebrew characters by Jewish communities and the use of Latin and Cyrillic scripts in Central Asia. A language's identity therefore cannot be reduced to a single alphabet. [s2]\n\nModern Persian writing runs from right to left. The University of Texas alphabet resource presents characters including pe, che, zhe and gaf alongside letters shared with Arabic writing. For a newcomer, recognizing the direction of writing and the inventory of letters is a practical first step; understanding the language requires learning how those letters function within Persian words and sentences. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ezafe",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A small connector with a large role",
        "content": "One characteristic construction is *ezafe*, a linking sound connecting a noun to a following description, possessor or name. In *ketab-e man*, meaning “my book,” the noun comes first and the possessor follows. The same principle links a noun with an adjective or connects a place designation with its name. [s4]\n\nThis construction explains why a Persian name or descriptive phrase may contain a short sound that is not obvious from an unmarked written text. After a consonant, the linking vowel is normally pronounced without being written as a full letter. Its written treatment changes after vowels. Reading aloud consequently involves grammatical knowledge as well as recognition of individual characters. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sentence-and-perspective",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Listening for the end of the sentence",
        "content": "Basic Persian sentences commonly place the verb at the end. The University of Texas teaching examples build from “I went” to a sentence meaning “I went to school yesterday,” with the verb following the other information. They also show that the placement of a time expression can vary. This gives an English-speaking learner a useful starting point without making English word order the template for Persian. [s5]\n\nTaken together, these features offer complementary ways into the language. Historical stages explain continuity, scripts reveal changing literary environments, and constructions such as ezafe show how everyday meaning is assembled. Learning to distinguish those layers is more useful than treating Persian as either an unchanged ancient language or merely a script to decipher. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
