    "description": "Azerbaijani, also called Azeri, is an Oghuz Turkic language spoken in Iran and neighboring countries. The name must be distinguished from historical Adhari, an Iranian language documented in Azerbaijan before the spread of Turkic speech.",
    "tags": ["Azerbaijani", "Azeri", "Turkic languages", "Oghuz", "Iranian Azerbaijan", "Language contact"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:29:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:29:40Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Azerbaijani and Azeri in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Azerbaijani in Iran, its Oghuz Turkic relationships, regional variation, literary history and distinction from historical Adhari."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "names-and-family",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What Azerbaijani and Azeri mean",
        "content": "Azerbaijani and Azeri are commonly used names for an Oghuz Turkic language. Oghuz is the branch that also includes Turkish and Turkmen. Azerbaijani therefore belongs to a different language family from Persian, even though prolonged contact has connected their vocabularies and literary worlds. The French language institute Inalco describes Azerbaijani as part of a dialect continuum with Turkish, rather than a language separated from its neighbors by perfectly sharp boundaries. [s1]\n\nIn Iran, the designation *Torki*, meaning Turkish or Turkic in this context, also appears in descriptions of local speech. Readers should check what a source means by that name instead of automatically identifying it with the standard language of Turkey. Scholarly classifications distinguish Azerbaijani from other Turkic varieties in Iran while recognizing transitional forms between them. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geography-and-variation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional language with wider connections",
        "content": "Northwestern Iran is a central part of Azerbaijani's geographic setting. Gerhard Doerfer's account identifies East and West Azerbaijan as important areas of use and also records speech outside that core, including Galugah near the southeastern Caspian coast. Its description places Iranian varieties within a wider linguistic landscape extending across national borders. [s2]\n\nAzerbaijani should not become a catch-all label for every Turkic language in Iran. Doerfer distinguishes it from Turkmen, Khorasan Turkic and other Oghuz varieties, while emphasizing the difficulty of drawing some boundaries. A map colored by language name can be an introduction, but it cannot capture every transition in pronunciation, grammar or local usage. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "literature-and-writing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Literature across changing writing systems",
        "content": "Azerbaijani has a literary history extending well beyond contemporary national borders. Doerfer discusses Nasimi, Shah Ismail, who wrote under the name Khatai, and Fuzuli among important authors. Their inclusion helps explain why the language belongs within a history of Iranian culture as well as within the broader Turkic literary world. [s2]\n\nWriting practices differ across that wider world. Inalco describes the historical use of the Perso-Arabic alphabet, followed by Latin and Cyrillic alphabets in the territory of the Republic of Azerbaijan and a return to Latin there. It also identifies continuing Perso-Arabic use in Iran. These differences matter when approaching books or learning materials: the language name alone does not identify the script a reader will encounter. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-adhari",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The historical language behind a similar name",
        "content": "A separate terminology problem concerns *Adhari*, also rendered Azari or Azeri in historical scholarship. Ehsan Yarshater uses this name for the Iranian language of Azerbaijan before the spread of Turkish there. His account draws on medieval geographical and historical writing as well as surviving linguistic evidence. It does not classify that earlier language as an old stage of modern Turkic Azerbaijani. [s3]\n\nThe distinction is essential when comparing sources. An article about medieval Adhari and a contemporary Azerbaijani grammar can use similar English spellings while discussing languages from different families. This is a question of identifying the subject and evidence precisely; a language-family label does not by itself settle modern personal or collective identity. Keeping the historical and modern meanings explicit allows both to be discussed without erasing either tradition. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
